  <escalation>
    <trigger>The read-only live count of `sprk_document` rows where two columns of one root type are both set to DIFFERENT records (`sprk_matter` vs `sprk_relatedmatter`, `sprk_project` vs `sprk_relatedproject`, `sprk_workassignment` vs `sprk_relatedworkassignment`) is greater than 0. Rule (3) would then refuse a RegardingResolver manual pick under those documents for real users. STOP and report the count (ids redacted to the first 8 characters) with options per CLAUDE.md §6; do not weaken the rule.</trigger>
    <trigger>The merged C# `IntermediateRootColumns` cannot be expressed as literal rows (for example a row whose root is computed at runtime from configuration), is no longer public, or matching it would require changing `CORE_RECORD_ENTITIES`, `CHILD_RECORD_ENTITIES` or `CORE_ANCESTOR_LOOKUPS`. STOP: the access taxonomy is pinned on both sides and moving it is an owner decision.</trigger>
    <trigger>The consumer grep finds a runtime caller other than the RegardingResolver PCF (for example a code page or another PCF calling `buildTodoRegardingUpdate`), or a caller whose handling of the new `error` case (ambiguous target) would crash or block a host save. STOP and name the caller and the behaviour before shipping; adding that caller's rebuild to the manual gate is not enough on its own.</trigger>
    <trigger>The merged C# rule differs from the background in a way that changes behaviour (for example an ambiguous pair no longer fails closed, or a new status). STOP and report; mirror only after confirming which side is intended.</trigger>
    <trigger>A pre-existing test expectation (library, PCF or C#) has to change for a reason OTHER than the three intended behaviour changes in the notes (budget / report card / agreement derive; invoice / document derive from typed links; an ambiguous document refuses). STOP and report the test and the reason; it means the change reaches further than the mirror.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <justification>
    <existing>`CORE_ANCESTOR_LOOKUPS` (`PolymorphicResolverService.ts:886-903`) lists the four `sprk_regarding{core}` columns: the CHILD's stamp columns and, through `buildRegardingSelectionPayload` `:1193-1196`, part of its pre-clear set. It describes what is WRITTEN on the child, keyed by root. It does not describe which columns each intermediate is READ through, and it covers only 4 of the 9 intermediates (a grep of src/client for `sprk_relatedmatter` outside node_modules finds no TypeScript copy of the document link vocabulary). The server's table is C# `CoreAncestorResolver.IntermediateRootColumns` (task 156).</existing>
    <extension>The derivation function and its module are extended, not forked; the only new surface is one exported literal table (plus its row type) in the existing module, and two C# tests in the existing test class. Extending `CORE_ANCESTOR_LOOKUPS` instead was rejected: it is keyed by root and doubles as the pre-clear set, so adding the intermediates' typed columns would null a host's own `sprk_matter` / `sprk_project` lookups on every pick and change the write contract.</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>A to-do (or event, communication) picked under an invoice, document, budget or report card through the RegardingResolver PCF is saved without its root stamp: it is invisible to the root's principals and refuses every upload with `container_ancestor_stale` until the server re-stamps it. A pick under a document naming two different matters saves unstamped on the client while the server refuses to derive it. Once owner round 8 item 3 makes the root columns read-only on those forms, the picker is the only way a user sets a root, and it previews a different access answer from the one the server owns. Nothing fails when the two tables drift.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

